---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ReleaseCover from '../../components/ReleaseCover.astro';
import { PROJECTS } from '../../data/projects';
import { MAX_PERSON_REFERENCE, SITE_URL, WEBSITE_REFERENCE } from '../../data/site';

const project = PROJECTS.find((item) => item.slug === 'quantization-aware-distillation')!;
const title = 'QAD, Distilled';
const description = 'Can a 4-bit language model learn from the full-precision model it came from?';
const canonical = `${SITE_URL}/projects/quantization-aware-distillation`;
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': `${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: MAX_PERSON_REFERENCE,
  publisher: MAX_PERSON_REFERENCE,
  url: canonical,
  mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
  isPartOf: WEBSITE_REFERENCE,
  datePublished: '2026-09-09',
  dateModified: '2026-09-10',
};
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="story">
    <header class="hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="label">MC–05 · SEPTEMBER 2026 · LOCAL AI · MODEL DISTILLATION · <a href="/about">BY MAX MACCRATE</a></p>
        <h1><em>QAD,</em> distilled.</h1>
        <p class="intro">Can a 4-bit language model learn from the full-precision model it came from?</p>
        <p class="setup">Gemma 4 E2B · Q4_0 GGUF · local training · native evaluation</p>
      </div>
      <ReleaseCover project={project} />
    </header>

    <div class="body">
      <section class="lead">
        <p>Quantization makes a language model smaller by storing its weights with fewer bits, but that compression can change how it answers. Quantization-aware distillation gives the low-precision student a full-precision teacher while it learns, so it can adapt to that loss before deployment.</p>
        <p>In this experiment, the resulting Q4_0 model scored higher than ordinary post-training quantization on both ARC-Challenge and HellaSwag. The gain was 3.84 percentage points on ARC and 2.60 points on HellaSwag.</p>
        <div class="score-line" aria-label="QAD compared with ordinary PTQ">
          <div><small>ARC OVER PTQ</small><strong>+3.84</strong><span>percentage points</span></div>
          <div><small>HELLASWAG OVER PTQ</small><strong>+2.60</strong><span>percentage points</span></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="question">
        <p class="label">THE QUESTION</p>
        <h2>What if deployment is part of the lesson?</h2>
        <p><a href="https://www.liquid.ai/blog/qad" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Liquid AI's QAD work</a> made this question hard to ignore. Their low-bit models suggested that quantization did not have to be the last, destructive step in a model's life.</p>
        <p>Ordinary post-training quantization takes a finished model and squeezes it into a smaller numerical format. The model never gets to respond to the change. QAD reverses that relationship: a full-precision teacher guides a student that is learning through the quantizer it will eventually use.</p>
        <div class="flow"><span>F16 teacher</span><i>guides</i><span>quantized student</span><i>ships as</i><span>Q4_0 model</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="comparison">
        <p class="label">THE ANSWER</p>
        <h2>Three versions. One comparison.</h2>
        <p>These percentages come from the same native evaluation. ARC-Challenge used all 1,172 questions available to the run; HellaSwag used a 1,000-row sample.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>Matched native accuracy</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>ARC<br /><small>raw</small></th><th>ARC<br /><small>normalized</small></th><th>HellaSwag<br /><small>raw</small></th><th>HellaSwag<br /><small>normalized</small></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Original F16</th><td>48.04%</td><td>49.74%</td><td>44.20%</td><td>55.60%</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Ordinary PTQ Q4_0</th><td>43.00%</td><td>45.99%</td><td>41.80%</td><td>53.80%</td></tr>
              <tr class="qad-row"><th>QAD Q4_0</th><td>46.84%</td><td>48.46%</td><td>44.40%</td><td>57.10%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The evaluation found QAD scored higher than ordinary PTQ across all four reported measures. On ARC, it correctly answered 45 of the 59 questions that F16 got right and ordinary PTQ missed. On raw HellaSwag, QAD and F16 were effectively tied.</p>
        <p class="note"><b>Normalized</b> is a second scoring rule on the same questions. It divides each answer's likelihood by its character length before ranking the choices.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="experiment">
        <p class="label">THE EXPERIMENT</p>
        <h2>Train for the model you plan to ship.</h2>
        <p>The training run used full-language QAD on Gemma 4 E2B, with the student learning while the deployment quantizer was in the loop. The resulting model was exported as Q4_0 GGUF and evaluated in the native runtime.</p>
        <div class="artifact"><span><small>TRAINING DATA</small><b>100,262 tokens</b></span><span><small>DEPLOYMENT</small><b>Q4_0 GGUF</b></span><span><small>EVALUATION</small><b>2,172 questions</b></span></div>
        <p class="note">The run used 100,262 rendered training tokens, including 83,004 supervised tokens. That is the scale of this result—not a claim about how much data every model or task needs.</p>
        <p class="note"><a href="https://github.com/mmaccrate/maccrate.ai" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View the maccrate.ai repository on GitHub.</a> <a href="https://huggingface.co/mmaccrate/gemma-4-E2B-it-QAD-GGUF" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Download the QAD GGUF from Hugging Face.</a></p>
      </section>

      <section class="limits">
        <p class="label">WHERE IT STOPS</p>
        <h2>A stronger 4-bit result, not a victory lap.</h2>
        <p>This was one seed and a retrospective matched evaluation on ARC-Challenge and HellaSwag. It does not show that QAD is generally better than the original F16 model, and it does not replace broader tests or a second confirmation run.</p>
        <p>In this experiment, training through the deployment format produced a Q4_0 model that scored higher than ordinary quantization on both tests.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <footer class="story-end"><a href="/">← Projects</a><a href="/about#contact">Contact →</a></footer>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>

